WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.389 --> 00:00:09.030
In Europe, we are facing
the worst energy crisis

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:10.773
in at least 50 years.

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:16.970
We compare this with the
first oil embargo in 1973,

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:19.710
but the current crisis
feels even worse than that

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:23.433
because this time, it's a
much wider energy crisis.

00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:28.380
The crisis is really huge
in terms of the price.

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:31.740
The price impacts homes, businesses,

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:33.780
industries, and the economy.

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:37.860
We see energy prices
breaking record after record.

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:39.870
The consequences for households

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:42.303
and companies are not sustainable anymore.

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:45.810
We've got less customers coming in.

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:47.730
Our margins are smaller.

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:52.730
It's a perfect storm for disaster.

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:57.540
European economies, they are
on the brink of recession,

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:01.683
so the winter is going
to be tough around here.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:03.930
Over the next few months,

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:06.900
Europe will face a high stakes puzzle,

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:09.690
how to keep the lights on
with a substantial portion

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:11.943
of its energy supply suddenly gone.

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:14.700
To do that, countries are going to need

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:17.640
to maneuver the balance of
supply and demand swiftly

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:20.310
and precisely and make hard decisions

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:24.183
about how to minimize the pain
of this winter and the next.

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:26.820
Beyond that, Europe could be forced

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:29.610
to reconsider its reliance on fossil fuels

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:31.500
in what some see as the beginning

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:34.563
of a fundamental transformation
of its energy system.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:40.050
The old pattern of responding
to every fossil fuel crisis

00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:42.660
with a grab for more fossil fuels

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:45.990
is simply not tenable anymore.

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:47.970
If there is to be a silver lining

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:51.690
from this terrible traumatic
year here in Europe,

00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:55.473
it is that it has reset
the energy debate forever.

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:06.150
The history of Europe and
Russia on gas goes back

00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:08.790
to the Cold War when Russia was not Russia

00:02:08.790 --> 00:02:10.143
but the Soviet Union.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:16.751
Slowly, Europe got in some ways addicted

00:02:16.751 --> 00:02:19.950
to cheap Soviet and Russian gas.

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:23.880
It was plentiful, it was
affordable, was around the corner,

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:26.271
and it really became the backbone

00:02:26.271 --> 00:02:28.743
of the European industrialization.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.080
Europe uses gas for heating,
for industrial production,

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:36.960
as a feed stock, and as a energy source.

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:39.270
It uses gas for power production.

00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:41.460
And natural gas is
really, really important

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:43.203
for these economies.

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:47.130
Flows of Russian gas to
Europe only increased

00:02:47.130 --> 00:02:49.650
after Putin became president in 2000

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:52.110
and doubled down on Russian gas production

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:55.050
via the state owned
energy company, Gazprom.

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:56.400
It's this gasfield

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:59.400
but it's the basis for
Gazprom's power and wealth

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:02.943
and in turn Russia's revival
as a power on the world stage.

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:07.530
By 2021, the EU was getting more than 40%

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:10.410
of its natural gas imports from Russia.

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:13.530
But looming behind all that cheap energy,

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:14.820
there was a problem.

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:17.940
While Russian gas was
cheap, it was also a tool

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:22.260
for the Kremlin to exert
political and economic pressure.

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:24.030
In this particular crisis,

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:27.140
Vladimir Putin saw the
opportunity to use gas

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:29.613
as that tool to put pressure in Europe.

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:33.910
For a lot of people,
the energy crisis began

00:03:33.910 --> 00:03:37.050
when Russia invaded Ukraine in February.

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:40.950
But for us in the energy
market, it began before that.

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:43.740
September, October of a year ago,

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:47.010
that's when Russia started
to reduce supply of gas.

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:49.470
Europe is facing a worsening energy crisis

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:50.550
ahead of winter.

00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:52.324
Gas prices have reached record highs

00:03:52.324 --> 00:03:55.440
and supplies are running
low across the block.

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:59.580
Russia blamed difficulties,
it blamed problems,

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:03.390
but surely, it was cutting
supply into Europe.

00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:06.060
That meant that prices starting to go up,

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:08.490
reaching very soon record highs.

00:04:08.490 --> 00:04:11.730
The situation got much worse into 2022,

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:14.043
particularly after the
invasion of Ukraine.

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:18.270
Air raid sirens in Kyiv,

00:04:18.270 --> 00:04:20.370
signaling that the full scale invasion

00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:22.983
of a European country was well underway.

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:29.310
We saw prices skyrocketing
because everybody was afraid

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:33.630
of how the battle would
actually impact infrastructure.

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:35.258
And then we started to see

00:04:35.258 --> 00:04:38.760
the western countries imposing sanctions.

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:41.430
Putin chose this war,

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:44.313
and now, he and his country
will bear the consequences.

00:04:45.330 --> 00:04:48.450
That's when we first
started seeing supply cuts

00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:50.943
from Russia to European countries.

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:54.300
Some hope Russia would
continue exporting gas

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:56.370
to Europe in spite of the war.

00:04:56.370 --> 00:04:59.193
But over the course of
the year, that hope faded.

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:02.700
Supplies were gradually cut

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:04.710
over the summer on Nord Stream,

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:08.670
the big gas pipeline that brings
fuel from Russia to Europe,

00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:10.860
and then finally stopped.

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:14.010
And the market realized
that really those supplies

00:05:14.010 --> 00:05:16.350
probably weren't going to come back.

00:05:16.350 --> 00:05:19.260
By late August, natural gas futures spiked

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:23.190
to more than seven times what
they had been a year before.

00:05:23.190 --> 00:05:25.140
Then, this happened.

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:26.370
Sabotage at sea.

00:05:26.370 --> 00:05:28.260
That's what President
Biden is calling the leaks

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:30.840
and explosions on the
Nord Stream pipelines.

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:34.943
This is natural gas, highly
flammable natural gas

00:05:34.943 --> 00:05:37.770
that is bubbling to the surface.

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:40.730
It remains unclear who carried
out the suspected sabotage

00:05:40.730 --> 00:05:43.350
of the Nord Stream pipelines and why,

00:05:43.350 --> 00:05:45.780
but the leaks sent an unmistakable message

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:49.500
about the vulnerability of
Europe's energy infrastructure.

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:51.270
It was seen as the start

00:05:51.270 --> 00:05:54.270
of a new chapter in this energy war.

00:05:54.270 --> 00:05:58.101
Everyone then thought, "What
if it's not a Russian pipeline

00:05:58.101 --> 00:06:00.685
but it's a pipeline from Norway

00:06:00.685 --> 00:06:04.410
into the European Union
bringing gas supply

00:06:04.410 --> 00:06:05.310
from the Nord Stream?"

00:06:05.310 --> 00:06:07.320
If that was hit,

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:11.040
Europe simply will not
have not nearly enough gas,

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:13.353
not even for the winter,
even for the fall.

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:16.890
Now, Europe is headed

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:19.743
into winter with sharply
reduced energy imports.

00:06:20.610 --> 00:06:22.350
Whether some countries
will make it through

00:06:22.350 --> 00:06:25.440
without severe power cuts
will depend on whether supply

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:29.433
and demand can be balanced
and on how cold it gets.

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:32.880
For some of the most vulnerable citizens,

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:35.700
the stakes could be life or death.

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:37.500
If we have more people unable

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:40.740
to afford to heat their homes
and sitting in the cold,

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:44.160
then experts expect
there to be more deaths.

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:46.631
Below 13 degrees Celsius

00:06:46.631 --> 00:06:49.590
is when you can have heart problems.

00:06:49.590 --> 00:06:52.980
You know, these are problems
that affect more the elderly

00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:54.840
or people with existing conditions.

00:06:54.840 --> 00:06:58.263
However, cold does kill people.

00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:02.400
And it worries me for the economy,

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:04.440
you know, for the European economy.

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:06.270
You know, we have not enough energy,

00:07:06.270 --> 00:07:09.795
rising interest rates, huge inflation.

00:07:09.795 --> 00:07:12.360
Energy, this crisis has revealed,

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:14.973
is so integral to everything.

00:07:16.230 --> 00:07:18.990
In hopes of averting
a worse case scenario,

00:07:18.990 --> 00:07:21.033
European governments are taking action,

00:07:21.870 --> 00:07:25.953
addressing both sides of the
dilemma, supply and demand.

00:07:27.990 --> 00:07:31.140
On the supply side, it's
trying to grab as much gas

00:07:31.140 --> 00:07:33.840
as they can find in the
international market,

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:35.910
particularly of liquified natural gas.

00:07:35.910 --> 00:07:39.390
This is the big gas
carriers that shape gas

00:07:39.390 --> 00:07:42.660
from places like Qatar
and the United States.

00:07:42.660 --> 00:07:46.770
Liquified natural gas
has really been the hero.

00:07:46.770 --> 00:07:51.770
We've seen a very strong flow
of LNG entering our ports,

00:07:52.290 --> 00:07:53.580
in our terminals.

00:07:53.580 --> 00:07:56.917
The problem is that
Europe now faces actually

00:07:56.917 --> 00:08:01.917
a lack of infrastructure
to import that gas.

00:08:02.310 --> 00:08:05.820
We can import gas from elsewhere,

00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:07.860
but we are very limited by the amount

00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:11.340
of LNG import facilities
that we have in place.

00:08:11.340 --> 00:08:13.470
And at the moment across Europe,

00:08:13.470 --> 00:08:17.070
there are 25 of these
facilities being planned.

00:08:17.070 --> 00:08:19.140
We'll have the first couple this winter,

00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:21.810
but through next year, we'll have more,

00:08:21.810 --> 00:08:24.120
and that will definitely help.

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:26.190
But to really get imports scaled up,

00:08:26.190 --> 00:08:27.660
new construction will be needed,

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:31.710
not just on the European side,
but on the US side as well.

00:08:31.710 --> 00:08:36.450
One of the dates that people
look forward to is 2026

00:08:36.450 --> 00:08:41.450
when a lot more US export
LNG capacity comes online.

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:43.980
That is a long time away

00:08:43.980 --> 00:08:46.410
and that's the problem with
building infrastructure.

00:08:46.410 --> 00:08:47.733
It takes time.

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:52.410
In the meantime, other more
contentious sources of energy

00:08:52.410 --> 00:08:54.160
will have to make up the shortfall.

00:08:55.830 --> 00:08:58.117
The energy crisis in Europe has prompted

00:08:58.117 --> 00:09:00.693
what we are calling a coal comeback.

00:09:01.950 --> 00:09:04.410
These countries were
really trying to get rid

00:09:04.410 --> 00:09:08.130
of the most pollutant source of energy,

00:09:08.130 --> 00:09:11.883
and in the short term at least,
it won't be able to do so.

00:09:13.110 --> 00:09:14.550
In Germany, which has struggled

00:09:14.550 --> 00:09:16.547
to phase out coal in recent years,

00:09:16.547 --> 00:09:19.230
the energy crisis has
prolonged the practice

00:09:19.230 --> 00:09:21.630
of razing small villages to mine

00:09:21.630 --> 00:09:24.390
the lignite coal buried underneath.

00:09:24.390 --> 00:09:26.070
Immerath was flattened to allow

00:09:26.070 --> 00:09:30.390
for the expansion of this
massive open cast coal mine

00:09:30.390 --> 00:09:32.250
which supplies a local power station,

00:09:32.250 --> 00:09:36.030
that is one of the most
polluting in the whole of Europe.

00:09:36.030 --> 00:09:37.470
So in the short term,

00:09:37.470 --> 00:09:40.860
we are going to see
probably emissions rising,

00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:44.010
and this can really
slow down European plans

00:09:44.010 --> 00:09:46.533
to become climate neutral.

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:52.389
We are seeing also a nuclear
comeback in some countries.

00:09:52.389 --> 00:09:54.189
Even in Germany

00:09:54.189 --> 00:09:57.895
where nuclear has been such a taboo,

00:09:57.895 --> 00:10:01.823
now the government has
decided to extend the life

00:10:01.823 --> 00:10:06.120
of its remaining nuclear power plants.

00:10:06.120 --> 00:10:07.980
Countries like the UK and Sweden

00:10:07.980 --> 00:10:11.130
are also hoping to bring
more nuclear power online.

00:10:11.130 --> 00:10:14.103
Though, any new plants are
years away from operation.

00:10:14.940 --> 00:10:16.170
In the short term though,

00:10:16.170 --> 00:10:19.560
nuclear is proving more of
a hindrance than a help.

00:10:19.560 --> 00:10:22.140
In a case of spectacularly bad timing,

00:10:22.140 --> 00:10:24.960
about half of France's
massive nuclear fleet

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:27.330
is currently shut down for repairs.

00:10:27.330 --> 00:10:30.330
Normally with that huge
fleet of nuclear energy,

00:10:30.330 --> 00:10:32.160
France has been a proud exporter

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:34.380
of power to the rest of Europe.

00:10:34.380 --> 00:10:37.860
They're now expecting to
need imports of electricity,

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:39.180
and that changes the dynamic

00:10:39.180 --> 00:10:41.610
for all the countries surrounding France.

00:10:41.610 --> 00:10:44.490
So the UK, Germany, Spain,

00:10:44.490 --> 00:10:47.580
everybody is going to have
to send imports to France,

00:10:47.580 --> 00:10:50.853
and that means that they've got
less electricity themselves.

00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:53.850
With no immediate way for Europe

00:10:53.850 --> 00:10:56.190
to make up the shortfall in energy supply,

00:10:56.190 --> 00:10:59.160
the other side of the
equation comes into play,

00:10:59.160 --> 00:11:00.423
reducing demand.

00:11:02.160 --> 00:11:04.770
Many countries have drawn
up guidelines for households

00:11:04.770 --> 00:11:07.860
and businesses to voluntarily
reduce their energy use,

00:11:07.860 --> 00:11:10.140
such as limiting heating in offices,

00:11:10.140 --> 00:11:11.700
turning off lights at night,

00:11:11.700 --> 00:11:15.540
and using clothes dryers
only at certain times of day.

00:11:15.540 --> 00:11:17.591
The single most important measure

00:11:17.591 --> 00:11:19.630
that European families can take

00:11:19.630 --> 00:11:22.380
is reduce the thermostat down.

00:11:22.380 --> 00:11:24.840
If we reduce the thermostat to 19,

00:11:24.840 --> 00:11:26.610
probably something like 5 to 7%

00:11:26.610 --> 00:11:29.373
of European gas demand could come off.

00:11:31.260 --> 00:11:33.840
But much of the necessary
reduction in energy demand

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:36.570
won't need any special
push from governments.

00:11:36.570 --> 00:11:40.050
It'll inevitably happen simply
because prices of energy

00:11:40.050 --> 00:11:43.110
and everything else are so high.

00:11:43.110 --> 00:11:47.130
We are seeing some
companies reducing output.

00:11:47.130 --> 00:11:51.180
Some of them might not
ever be able to get back

00:11:51.180 --> 00:11:54.603
to the production levels
from before the crisis.

00:11:55.800 --> 00:11:59.533
Most of all the products
have risen in price.

00:11:59.533 --> 00:12:04.533
I'm saying everything's
up, minimum 15, 20%.

00:12:05.100 --> 00:12:09.330
Our margins are getting
smaller, but also the footfall

00:12:09.330 --> 00:12:11.850
of customers coming in is getting smaller.

00:12:11.850 --> 00:12:14.040
The people need more money to live

00:12:14.040 --> 00:12:16.230
'cause everything's going up.

00:12:16.230 --> 00:12:18.630
You know, like you say,
a lot of businesses,

00:12:18.630 --> 00:12:20.073
they'll just call it a day.

00:12:25.710 --> 00:12:28.140
Europeans are also
feeling the extra squeeze

00:12:28.140 --> 00:12:29.790
from their home energy bills,

00:12:29.790 --> 00:12:31.920
creating a dire economic situation

00:12:31.920 --> 00:12:34.200
for many low and middle income people

00:12:34.200 --> 00:12:37.710
and sparking cost of living
protests throughout Europe.

00:12:37.710 --> 00:12:40.680
Don't pay UK means don't
pay your energy bills.

00:12:40.680 --> 00:12:43.233
Our goal is to get a
million people striking.

00:12:53.296 --> 00:12:55.170
In response, some countries have decided

00:12:55.170 --> 00:12:56.460
to partially subsidize

00:12:56.460 --> 00:12:58.710
their citizens' heating
and electric bills,

00:12:58.710 --> 00:13:01.380
which could cost governments
hundreds of billions of euros

00:13:01.380 --> 00:13:03.333
or pounds for this winter alone.

00:13:04.260 --> 00:13:05.790
It also introduces problems

00:13:05.790 --> 00:13:08.220
for the supply side of the equation.

00:13:08.220 --> 00:13:11.370
The problem with the
subsidies and the price caps

00:13:11.370 --> 00:13:15.120
for the families is that
they stop the price signal

00:13:15.120 --> 00:13:17.550
from reaching household, and it means

00:13:17.550 --> 00:13:20.430
that families do not have
the incentive to save,

00:13:20.430 --> 00:13:23.430
to economize, to try to consume less

00:13:23.430 --> 00:13:24.900
than they did last winter.

00:13:24.900 --> 00:13:28.233
So it's a very difficult
and precarious equilibrium

00:13:28.233 --> 00:13:30.430
in between helping families

00:13:30.430 --> 00:13:33.310
and shielding them from
the impact of high prices

00:13:33.310 --> 00:13:37.737
and letting the market
work and reduce demand

00:13:37.737 --> 00:13:41.610
so there is enough gas to
make it through the winter.

00:13:41.610 --> 00:13:43.020
One of the things that you could do

00:13:43.020 --> 00:13:45.180
is protect the most vulnerable families,

00:13:45.180 --> 00:13:47.190
the poor, the working class,

00:13:47.190 --> 00:13:49.080
but at the moment, most European countries

00:13:49.080 --> 00:13:51.393
is just literally protecting everyone.

00:13:54.630 --> 00:13:56.790
Europe may not have found
the perfect solution

00:13:56.790 --> 00:14:00.150
to the supply and demand puzzle,
but as winter approaches,

00:14:00.150 --> 00:14:02.790
it's finally caught a few breaks.

00:14:02.790 --> 00:14:04.770
Many countries have successfully filled up

00:14:04.770 --> 00:14:06.960
their emergency stores of natural gas,

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:10.616
bringing gas prices down
from their late summer peak,

00:14:10.616 --> 00:14:12.360
and forecasts are predicting

00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:14.403
relatively mild winter temperatures.

00:14:15.360 --> 00:14:17.100
Probably, Europe will be able to make it

00:14:17.100 --> 00:14:18.210
through the winter.

00:14:18.210 --> 00:14:20.775
The problem is what
happened next year into 2023

00:14:20.775 --> 00:14:23.908
and the winter of 2023, 2024.

00:14:25.560 --> 00:14:27.817
Without any Russian gas next year,

00:14:27.817 --> 00:14:31.620
next winter looks absolutely horrendous.

00:14:31.620 --> 00:14:34.835
There is not enough gas
out there in the LNG market

00:14:34.835 --> 00:14:38.973
to replace imports of
Russia gas for a full year.

00:14:39.960 --> 00:14:41.820
This is a permanent change

00:14:41.820 --> 00:14:44.340
in the way that Europe has bought gas,

00:14:44.340 --> 00:14:47.523
and that means permanent
higher gas and oil prices.

00:14:48.780 --> 00:14:51.360
Many are hoping that
change can push Europe

00:14:51.360 --> 00:14:53.610
in a direction it needs to go anyway

00:14:53.610 --> 00:14:56.160
towards more renewable energy.

00:14:56.160 --> 00:14:59.520
In light of this year's crisis,
a push towards renewables,

00:14:59.520 --> 00:15:02.617
electrification, and energy
efficiency is more attractive

00:15:02.617 --> 00:15:04.770
than it's ever been.

00:15:04.770 --> 00:15:08.720
What we are seeing this
year is that fossil fuels

00:15:08.720 --> 00:15:13.380
are not the way to have
a secure energy system.

00:15:13.380 --> 00:15:17.353
That actually, clean energy
with renewables at its heart

00:15:17.353 --> 00:15:21.030
are also going to be more secure.

00:15:21.030 --> 00:15:24.240
It's not easy to roll out a
secure clean energy system.

00:15:24.240 --> 00:15:25.170
Very, very difficult.

00:15:25.170 --> 00:15:28.800
Lots and lots of work to
do, innovation to happen,

00:15:28.800 --> 00:15:32.460
investment to happen, but it can be done.

00:15:32.460 --> 00:15:35.430
Renewables are already
helping to turn the tide.

00:15:35.430 --> 00:15:36.810
A study found that the wind

00:15:36.810 --> 00:15:39.210
and solar capacity that
was added between March

00:15:39.210 --> 00:15:41.940
and September alone saved
Europe the equivalent

00:15:41.940 --> 00:15:45.930
of $11 billion in avoided gas imports.

00:15:45.930 --> 00:15:47.490
We are seeing that authorities,

00:15:47.490 --> 00:15:50.910
governments are raising
incentives for the construction

00:15:50.910 --> 00:15:55.170
of more clean, wind, solar, power plants.

00:15:55.170 --> 00:15:57.833
And Europe still is promising

00:15:57.833 --> 00:16:02.833
and targeting to be climate
neutral in the mid-century.

00:16:03.810 --> 00:16:06.120
Whilst fossil fuels have, of course,

00:16:06.120 --> 00:16:09.568
powered incredible improvements
in living standards,

00:16:09.568 --> 00:16:14.568
they've also led to war after
war, crisis after crisis.

00:16:14.970 --> 00:16:18.450
But in the past, we've had no alternative.

00:16:18.450 --> 00:16:20.537
This time, we have line of sight

00:16:20.537 --> 00:16:25.537
towards an energy system
that is sustainable,

00:16:25.680 --> 00:16:28.473
affordable, and secure.

00:16:29.340 --> 00:16:31.080
And if there's going to be anything good

00:16:31.080 --> 00:16:32.460
coming out of this year,

00:16:32.460 --> 00:16:35.973
it is that vision and
accelerating towards it.

